title: lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/tw5/tiddlers/sigil-has
tags: $:/tags/Global lar:///ha.ka.ba/tags/SharktoothSigil
lar-kind: relation
lar-pono-uri: lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot
lar-pattern: <<~\s*has\s+entity=`([\w-]+)`(?:\s+name=`([\w.-]+)`)?(?:\s+params=`([^`]*)`)?\s*>>
lar-example: <<~ has entity=`rank` name=`pulse` params=`levels/0-4 reads/a-mark-a-morpheme-a-word`>>
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

<!-- has — an ENTITY, a NAME, and what it HOLDS. The boot seed writes the relation and this shelf
     answers for it everywhere else:

         <<~ has entity=`rank` name=`pulse` params=`levels/0-4 reads/a-mark-a-morpheme-a-word`>>

     Thirty-five carriers each declared a local shorthand for exactly this, hard-coding the entity
     into the procedure's own name — seventy-two names, one shape. The entity rides as the NAMED
     `entity` here, so one definition answers for all of them.

     WHAT AN ENTITY HOLDS RIDES TICKED. It carries slashes, colons, arrows and prose; one pair of ticks
     makes the whole run ONE value, and the arrows inside it stay.

     AND IT RIDES AS TEXT. What an entity holds carries square brackets, arrows and backticks — a
     filtered transclusion renders each as a LINK to a tiddler nobody wrote, and wikitext would read
     `[Fiduciary: POA]` as a wikilink. A value names no address. -->

\procedure ~has(entity:"" name:"" params:"")
<span class="lar-has"><<entity>> <<name>> holds [<$text text=<<params>>/>]</span>
\end
